one of the most impressive pieces of  next generation technology  is the playstation 5's dual sense  controller it's decked out with all  sorts of tech bells and whistles that  can augment gameplay  when it's fully utilized the dual sense  is maybe the most exciting thing about  the ps5 and the thing that's most likely  to alter the way we experience games  you get the best sense of what the dual  sense can do with astro's playroom  the 3d platformer that comes bundled  with the ps5  astros playroom goes wild in showing off  the controller's features  it places a big emphasis on haptic  feedback the tactile sensations created  by the controller's vibrations and  rumbles  it also does a lot with the controller's  adaptive triggers and utilizes its touch  pad  internal gyroscope and built-in  microphone all of those elements  work to transform astro from a fairly  standard platformer  into something much more interesting  let's start with the haptics  the dual sense features enhanced  rumbling that doesn't just shake the  whole controller but can pinpoint  sensations in different places on the  gamepad  pretty much everything you do in astros  playroom produces some haptic feedback  from punching enemies to just walking  across surfaces when astro's robotic  metal feet hit a metal surface you feel  the feedback with every step  alternating on different sides of the  controller skating across  ice produces a thinner vibration across  a larger area to give the sense of  gliding  and when it rains you get random  vibrations at points all over the  controller  with all that extra feedback you really  feel a lot closer to astros actions  even though you're just holding a  controller the haptics help you feel  what's going on in the game  while you see it next we've got the  adaptive triggers  the triggers on the dual sense can  change their tension depending on the  situation to make using them feel more  realistic  so when astro picks up a bow and arrow  the trigger tension increases to mirror  the sensation of straining to pull back  on the string  it's a little touch that again makes  what you're doing with the controller  match what you're seeing on screen a  little more closely the dual sense also  includes some older features from past  playstation controllers  there's the big touchpad which you'll  use in astro by swiping your finger  across it to roll a ball  there's the gyroscope which lets you  tilt and move the dual sense to control  certain things  and there's the microphone and speaker  which make talking to your party a  little easier  the speaker is pretty great when used  with the haptics since the small  sounds of walking on metal or sand help  your brain make sense of what you're  feeling with the controller  the old controller features combined  with the new ones to create some fresh  experiences  astros playroom shows off just how cool  the dual sense can be  and it is pretty cool  if there's a drawback though it's that  so many of the features  might not get broad use in games  particularly those games that are  released  on multiple platforms take spider-man  miles morales as an example  like astro it's a first-party ps5 launch  game and it has some cool dual sense  specific features  the adaptive triggers set different  tensions for your webs as you swing  around new york or yank weapons out of  the hands of enemies  certain moments like when miles rides  the subway push the haptics in  interesting ways with the controller  simulating the field the tracks beneath  his feet  but spider-man definitely doesn't have  the dedication to the dual sense  that astro does which could be a result  of the fact that it's also coming out on  ps4  developer insomniac games has done some  things with the controller but those  things  don't really change the spider-man  experience sony has had technologically  impressive devices in the past that have  offered new opportunities for gameplay  but they've  often gone under-used by developers for  instance not many games really embraced  the dualshock 4's touch pad  light bar and gyroscope last generation  the playstation vita handheld sported a  touchpad  a touchscreen a camera and a microphone  all of those elements offered cool ways  to change gameplay but only a handful of  sony-backed games  really use them like astra's playroom  with the dual sense sony developer media  molecule released tearaway to show off  all the features of both the vita and  the dualshock 4 but that game didn't  inspire a wave of novel uses for the  hardware from  other developers as a piece of next-gen  technology  the dual sense comes with a lot of  possibilities the features it brings to  the table have the potential to offer  new avenues for gameplay and new ways  for us to connect with games  but that will only happen if developers  embrace the hardware  and there really hasn't been a great  track record of that over the last two  generations  if you're picking up a ps5 you should  play astros playroom and see what some  of this next gen tech is capable of  delivering  and hopefully we'll see more developers  inspired to adopt the dual senses  features as we get into the ps5  generation  you
 

